CAT = "sgresAgencyLease"; SEG = "AGL"
Q = [
("Agency can arise by express agreement, implication, necessity, estoppel and:",
 "Ratification", ["Novation", "Frustration", "Indemnity"],
 "Ratification is when a principal later adopts an act done on its behalf without authority. The adoption makes the act binding as if it had been authorised."),
("Under an exclusive estate agency agreement, the seller:",
 "Appoints one agent for a set period", ["May appoint many agents at once", "Pays no commission in any event", "Can never end the agreement before expiry"],
 "An exclusive agreement gives one estate agent the sole right to act for a fixed period. A non-exclusive agreement lets the client appoint several agents."),
("A registered salesperson may carry out estate agency work through:",
 "One estate agent at a time", ["Any number of agencies", "No agency, as a sole trader", "Two agencies if both agree"],
 "A salesperson must be registered with CEA under a licensed estate agent and can be registered with only one estate agent at any time."),
("A buyer relies on an agent's authority that the seller's own conduct led him to believe in. This is:",
 "Apparent authority", ["Express actual authority", "Agency of necessity", "Delegated authority"],
 "Apparent (ostensible) authority arises when the principal's words or conduct represent that the agent has authority. The principal can then be bound even if no actual authority was given."),
("A power of attorney allows the donee to:",
 "Act legally for the donor", ["Own the donor's property", "Avoid all stamp duty", "Act after the donor dies"],
 "A power of attorney authorises the donee to act on the donor's behalf, for example to sign a sale. It ends on the donor's death, when the estate is dealt with by an executor or administrator."),
("An agent acting for one seller quietly takes on a rival seller of a near-identical unit in the same block. Which common law duty is most at risk?",
 "Avoiding conflicts of interest", ["Keeping separate accounts", "Obeying lawful instructions", "Using reasonable skill"],
 "As a fiduciary, an agent must not put itself where its duty to one principal clashes with its duty to another or its own interest, unless it discloses this and gets informed consent."),
("An agent hands the client's whole job to another agency without consent. Which duty is breached?",
 "The duty not to delegate", ["The duty to keep accounts", "The duty to advertise", "The duty to indemnify"],
 "The principal chose the agent personally, so the agent may not delegate its authority without consent. This is the rule usually summed up as delegatus non potest delegare."),
("The main duty a principal owes the agent is to:",
 "Pay the agreed commission", ["Attend every viewing", "Pay the agent's CPD fees", "Register the agent with CEA"],
 "The principal must pay the agreed remuneration when it is earned and indemnify the agent for proper expenses incurred in carrying out the agency."),
("An agency relationship ends automatically on:",
 "The principal's death", ["A change in asking price", "The first viewing", "Renewal of the agent's licence"],
 "Agency ends by operation of law on the death, mental incapacity or bankruptcy of the principal, and also by expiry, completion or revocation."),
("Keeping the principal's money and records separate from the agent's own reflects the duty to:",
 "Account", ["Delegate", "Advertise", "Ratify"],
 "The agent must keep proper accounts and must not mix the principal's money or property with its own."),
("When a tenant assigns the lease, the landlord and the new tenant are bound to each other by:",
 "Privity of estate", ["Privity of contract", "A bare licence", "A trust"],
 "Privity of contract binds the original parties; privity of estate binds the current landlord and tenant. Covenants that touch and concern the land run with the lease to the assignee."),
("A tenant who stays on after the lease ends without the landlord's consent holds:",
 "A tenancy at sufferance", ["A tenancy at will", "A periodic tenancy", "A fixed-term lease"],
 "A tenancy at sufferance arises when a tenant holds over without the landlord's assent. If the landlord consents, it may become a tenancy at will or, once rent is accepted, a periodic tenancy."),
("A month-to-month tenancy that continues until either side gives notice is:",
 "A periodic tenancy", ["A fixed-term lease", "A tenancy at sufferance", "A licence"],
 "A periodic tenancy runs from period to period, such as monthly, until ended by proper notice. A fixed-term lease ends automatically when its term expires."),
("Which is an essential element of a lease?",
 "A term of certain duration", ["A registered caveat", "Furnished premises", "A stamped receipt"],
 "A lease needs identified premises, exclusive possession and a term whose start and maximum length are certain. Stamping and registration affect enforcement, not whether a lease exists."),
("When a tenant sublets the premises, the original tenant:",
 "Stays the landlord's tenant", ["Drops out of the lease", "Becomes the owner", "Loses all its liability"],
 "In a sublease the tenant keeps its own lease and grants a shorter one to the subtenant. It remains liable to the landlord, which is why most tenancy agreements need the landlord's consent to sublet."),
("In a novation of a lease agreed by landlord, outgoing tenant and incoming tenant, the outgoing tenant:",
 "Is released from the lease", ["Stays liable for all rent", "Becomes the sub-landlord", "Must pay the landlord's property tax"],
 "Novation replaces the old contract with a new one between the landlord and the incoming tenant, so the outgoing tenant is released. In an assignment, the original tenant can stay liable."),
("A covenant implied on the landlord in every lease is:",
 "Quiet enjoyment", ["Free repair of every item", "Payment of all utilities", "Rent that never rises"],
 "The landlord impliedly covenants that the tenant will enjoy possession without interference from the landlord. Repairs and utilities depend on the express terms."),
("Under the Distress Act 1934, a landlord owed rent may:",
 "Apply to court to seize goods", ["Lock the tenant out of the premises", "Cut off water and power", "Hold the tenant's passport"],
 "A landlord can apply to court for a writ of distress to seize and sell the tenant's goods for rent in arrears. Self-help measures such as lock-outs are not allowed."),
("An option to renew in a tenancy agreement allows the tenant to:",
 "Extend on agreed terms", ["End the lease at any time", "Buy the property", "Sublet without consent"],
 "An option to renew lets the tenant take a further term, usually on giving notice in time and on terms or a rent formula set out in the agreement."),
("For a serious breach of covenant, the landlord's remedy of ending the lease and retaking the premises is:",
 "Forfeiture by re-entry", ["Distress for rent", "Specific performance", "Novation"],
 "Forfeiture lets the landlord end the lease and recover possession where the lease gives a right of re-entry. The court can grant the tenant relief in some cases."),
]
